If your level changes via most means, your maxhp and maxpw change by a
value depending on your stats. However, if your level changes via
failed polyself ("you feel like a new man!", etc.), it's multiplied by
the ratio of the old level and the new level. This is mostly
reasonable; however, it can be abused by getting an abnormally high
maxhp and maxpw via some other means, and also a large stack of
life-saving, then repeatedly failed-polyselfing at level 1, putting on
a new life-saving amulet whenever your new form is not healthy enough to
survive, and draining yourself down to level 1 whenever you gain levels
from the failed polyself. A dedicated pudding farmer can quickly get
millions of maxhp and maxpw this way.

This is almost certainly a bug; there's some debate among variant
developers (who have to reconstruct the entire bug list more or less
from scratch, because neither detailed descriptions of bugs in 3.4.3,
nor the devteam's patches to fix them, are public), as to whether
SC343-10 refers to this bug, or whether it refers to a related bug
involving helms of brilliance and rings of gain constitution (which can
easily gain thousands of maxhp and maxpw, although not so much
millions). The one-liner descriptions in the bug list are not enough to
tell which is which (and, I'm sure, lead to the devteam getting a bunch
of duplicate bug reports, and possibly not getting bug reports they
should because people think they're already on the list; I know I've
submitted duplicate bugs before now because the bug list wasn't detailed
enough to tell whether they were duplicate or not).

